"08_resources.mst — how this port finds Dolphin's artwork.

 The chain the corpus already implements, once these four gaps are filled:

     ShellView icon                         ^##(self) defaultIcon
     ClassDescription >> defaultIcon        ^Icon fromId: self defaultIconName
     ClassDescription >> defaultIconName    ^File composeStem: self name
                                                 extension: '.ico'
     Image class >> fromId: anId            ^self fromId: anId
                                                in: SessionManager current
                                                     defaultResourceLibrary
     SessionManager >> defaultResLibPath    ^'DolphinDR8'

 so `ShellView icon` becomes `LoadImage(hDolphinDR8, 'ShellView.ico', ...)`.
 `resources/DolphinDR8.dll` is this port's ARM64 rebuild of Dolphin's own
 resource DLL, carrying the same 295 icons under the same names — see
 `resources/README.md`. Keeping the NAME is what makes `defaultResLibPath`
 work unaltered.

 `ShellView new create` raising `defaultIcon` was the visible end of this
 chain being absent; the error named the last link rather than the missing
 four."

"── WeakLookupTable ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 `ResourceLibrary class >> initialize` builds its `Libraries` alias table with
 one. A plain LookupTable here: the table maps a library stem to an open
 ResourceLibrary, and this port WANTS those retained — an open resource
 module is exactly the thing that should not be collected while the image is
 running. The weakness in Dolphin's version is about image stripping, which
 this port does not do.

 Same reasoning, and the same recorded caveat, as `SharedLookupTable` in
 07_dialogs.mst."
LookupTable subclass: WeakLookupTable [ ]

"── File: the path-splitting Dolphin's code calls ──────────────────────
 Not Dolphin's File class — that is the whole file-system layer, and nothing
 here opens a file. These are the four PURE STRING operations the resource
 path needs, and their exact semantics were taken from the oracle rather than
 assumed, because the edge cases are where a reimplementation goes wrong:

     File composeStem: 'ShellView' extension: '.ico'  ->  'ShellView.ico'
     File splitStemFrom: 'C:\\dir\\DolphinDR8.dll'    ->  'DolphinDR8'
     File splitPathFrom: 'C:\\dir\\DolphinDR8.dll'    ->  'C:\\dir\\'
     File splitPathFrom: 'DolphinDR8'                 ->  ''
     File splitFilenameFrom: 'C:\\dir\\DolphinDR8.dll' -> 'DolphinDR8.dll'

 Note the trailing separator `splitPathFrom:` keeps, and that
 `composeStem:extension:` is plain concatenation — the extension carries its
 own dot."
Object subclass: File [
    File class >> _lastSeparatorIn: aString [
        | last |
        last := 0.
        1 to: aString size do: [ :i |
            | c |
            c := aString at: i.
            (c = $\ or: [ c = $/ ]) ifTrue: [ last := i ] ].
        ^last
    ]
    File class >> splitPathFrom: aString [
        | last |
        last := self _lastSeparatorIn: aString.
        ^last = 0 ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ aString copyFrom: 1 to: last ]
    ]
    File class >> splitFilenameFrom: aString [
        | last |
        last := self _lastSeparatorIn: aString.
        ^last = 0 ifTrue: [ aString ]
                  ifFalse: [ aString copyFrom: last + 1 to: aString size ]
    ]
    File class >> splitStemFrom: aString [
        | fn dot |
        fn := self splitFilenameFrom: aString.
        dot := 0.
        1 to: fn size do: [ :i | (fn at: i) = $. ifTrue: [ dot := i ] ].
        ^dot = 0 ifTrue: [ fn ] ifFalse: [ fn copyFrom: 1 to: dot - 1 ]
    ]
    File class >> splitExtensionFrom: aString [
        | fn dot |
        fn := self splitFilenameFrom: aString.
        dot := 0.
        1 to: fn size do: [ :i | (fn at: i) = $. ifTrue: [ dot := i ] ].
        ^dot = 0 ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ fn copyFrom: dot + 1 to: fn size ]
    ]
    File class >> composeStem: aStem extension: anExtension [
        ^aStem , anExtension
    ]
]

"── FileLocator: the file FALLBACK, not the primary path ───────────────
 `ImageFromStringResourceInitializer>>tryLoadFromFile:extent:` is Dolphin's
 second attempt when a resource identifier is not in the module:

     ^anImage loadFromFile: (FileLocator default localFileSpecFor: identifier)

 Kept working because it is genuinely useful here — the extracted `.ico`
 files sit beside the DLL — and because leaving it as a send to nil would turn
 a missing icon into an unrelated error two classes away."
Object subclass: FileLocator [
    | base |
    FileLocator class >> on: aString [ ^self basicNew setBase: aString ]
    FileLocator class >> default [ ^self on: 'resources\icons\' ]
    FileLocator class >> imageRelative [ ^self on: 'resources\' ]
    FileLocator class >> installRelative [ ^self on: 'resources\' ]
    setBase: aString [ base := aString ]
    base [ ^base ]
    localFileSpecFor: aString [ ^base , aString ]
]

ResourceLibrary extend [
    "Dolphin's `defaultLoadFlags` answers 34 — LOAD_LIBRARY_AS_IMAGE_RESOURCE
     (16r20) | LOAD_LIBRARY_AS_DATAFILE (16r2) — which maps the module for
     resources only, executing nothing. Verified against our own DLL: the
     module handle and the HICON `LoadImage` returns are identical whether the
     library is opened with those flags or with none."
    loadFlags [ ^loadFlags ifNil: [ self class defaultLoadFlags ] ]

    "Dolphin searches working directory, then image-relative, then
     install-relative. This port's equivalent list, in the same spirit: the
     name as given, then `resources\\` (where the build puts it), then with
     `.dll` appended for each. `defaultResLibPath` answers a STEM
     ('DolphinDR8'), so the extension has to be tried."
    ResourceLibrary class >> searchSpecsFor: aString [
        | stem out |
        stem := aString.
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        out add: stem; add: stem , '.dll'.
        out add: 'resources\' , stem; add: 'resources\' , stem , '.dll'.
        ^out
    ]

    ResourceLibrary class >> open: aString [
        | lib |
        lib := self basicNew.
        lib fileName: aString.
        (self searchSpecsFor: aString) do: [ :spec |
            (lib attemptToOpen: spec) ifTrue: [ ^lib ] ].
        "Not found. Answer the unopened library rather than raising: Dolphin
         signals a Notification here and carries on, because a missing
         resource library must degrade to missing artwork and not to a dead
         image."
        ^lib
    ]
    ResourceLibrary class >> open: aString withAliases: anArray [
        ^self open: aString
    ]
    ResourceLibrary class >> open: aString withAliases: anArray flags: anInteger [
        ^self open: aString
    ]
]

"── the session's default library ──────────────────────────────────────
 Dolphin's own two methods, verbatim in behaviour. `defaultResLibPath`
 answering the bare stem is why our DLL keeps the name `DolphinDR8.dll`."
SessionManager extend [
    defaultResLibPath [ ^'DolphinDR8' ]
    defaultResourceLibrary [
        ^ResourceLibraryDefault ifNil: [
            ResourceLibraryDefault := ResourceLibrary open: self defaultResLibPath ]
    ]
    SessionManager class >> defaultResourceLibrary [
        ^SessionManager classVarCurrent defaultResourceLibrary
    ]
]

"── `defaultIcon`, Dolphin's loose ClassDescription methods ────────────
 Filed onto `Core.ClassDescription` by `Dolphin GDI Graphics.pax`, so they are
 sent to a CLASS — every `Foo class >> icon` in the corpus is
 `^##(self) defaultIcon`. This port has no ClassDescription; the class side of
 `Object` is the equivalent reach, and the bodies are Dolphin's."
nil subclass: Object [
    Object class >> defaultIconName [
        ^File composeStem: self name extension: '.ico'
    ]
    Object class >> defaultIcon [
        ^Icon fromId: self defaultIconName
    ]

    "`self name` INSIDE a class-side method. `Behavior>>name` exists and a
     DYNAMIC send to a class finds it — `each name` in
     `DolphinBoot class >> initializeViewClasses` does exactly that — but
     `self name` written in a class method compiles to a class-side send,
     which looks for `ShellView class >> name` and misses. The asymmetry is
     the port's, not Dolphin's, and the corpus writes `self name` in class
     methods freely, so it is answered once here rather than per caller.

     `printString` of a class answers its bare name in this port (measured:
     `DisplayMonitor printString` -> 'DisplayMonitor'). Dolphin answers the
     Symbol `#ShellView`, and both concatenate to the same 'ShellView.ico'."
    Object class >> name [ ^self printString ]
]

"── marshalling the resource name ──────────────────────────────────────
 Dolphin's `Image>>loadResource:fromModule:extent:flags:` is

     ^User32 loadImage: moduleObject asParameter
             lpszName: identifierObject asResourceId
             ...

 and in Dolphin a String is directly passable as an LPCTSTR. This port's FFI
 floor refuses that outright — `FFI: cannot pass String as a pointer argument`
 — which is the floor doing its job rather than guessing an encoding.

 So the send is overridden ONLY to marshal, and every other part of the
 method is Dolphin's, including the `bitOr: 64` (LR_DEFAULTSIZE) it adds when
 no extent was asked for.

 An INTEGER identifier must not be marshalled: `MAKEINTRESOURCE` passes the
 ordinal AS the pointer value, which is why `Integer>>asResourceId` answers
 self and why the two cases are separated here rather than in `asResourceId`.

 The buffer is freed with `ensure:`, because this runs once per icon per
 image and a leak here is a leak per window."
Image extend [
    loadResource: identifierObject fromModule: moduleObject extent: aPoint flags: anInteger [
        | id flags |
        id := identifierObject asResourceId.
        flags := (aPoint = Point classVarZero)
            ifTrue: [ anInteger bitOr: 64 ]
            ifFalse: [ anInteger ].
        id isString ifFalse: [
            ^User32
                loadImage: moduleObject asParameter
                lpszName: id
                uType: self imageType
                cxDesired: aPoint x
                cyDesired: aPoint y
                fuLoad: flags ].
        ^[ | buf |
           buf := Utf16Buffer fromString: id.
           [ User32
                loadImage: moduleObject asParameter
                lpszName: buf
                uType: self imageType
                cxDesired: aPoint x
                cyDesired: aPoint y
                fuLoad: flags ] ensure: [ buf free ] ] value
    ]
]

"── asResourceId ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 `Image>>loadResource:fromModule:extent:flags:` normalises its identifier
 with this before calling LoadImage. Dolphin's `Integer>>asResourceId`
 answers self — an integer ID passes through as MAKEINTRESOURCE — and the
 String version LOWERCASES, which the oracle settled:

     'ShellView.ico' asResourceId          ->  'shellview.ico'
     'ShellView.ico' asResourceId == ...   ->  false   (a new string)

 Harmless for lookup, since Windows compares resource names
 case-insensitively (measured against our own DLL: SHELLVIEW.ICO,
 ShellView.ico and shellview.ico all answer the same HICON), but reproduced
 rather than skipped because the identifier is also a CACHE KEY — Dolphin
 interns images by it, and two spellings of one icon must not become two
 entries."
String extend [
    asResourceId [ ^self asLowercase ]
]
Integer extend [
    asResourceId [ ^self ]
]
